## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-region AWS deployments using Terraform provider aliases and proper resource binding to specific providers.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform provider aliases for multiple AWS regions
- Resource addressing with provider assignments
- Cross-region planning and deployment validation

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "west"
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_vpc" "east_vpc" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
}

resource "aws_nat_gateway" "west_nat" {
  provider     = aws.west
  allocation_id = aws_eip.nat_eip.id
  subnet_id    = aws_subnet.west_subnet.id
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to enforce cross-provider tagging policy across environments using plan JSON, with CI gating and explicit drift handling.

## Key Concepts
- plan JSON parsing across AWS and GCP
- per-env backends and state isolation
- gating deployments behind approvals
- drift detection via re-plan and re-validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js validator skeleton
const plan = require('./plan.json');
let ok = true;
(plan.resource_changes || []).forEach(r => {
  const tags = (r.change?.after?.tags) || {};
  if (!tags.CostCenter || !tags.Env) ok = false;
});
process.exit(ok ? 0 : 1);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would policy checks adapt to Terraform Cloud/Enterprise?
- How handle resources with dynamic tags or provider-specific nuances?
